  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A strong cold front will bring the next round of \nshowers and thunderstorms Tuesday through Tuesday night. Some storms \nmay be severe.\n\nA mid/upper level trough will dig across the Great Lakes region \nthrough Tuesday night. A surface low in northern Ontario will pivot \naround the southern shore of James Bay while a cold front moves \nacross the eastern Great Lakes region Tuesday night. \n\nShowers and possibly a few thunderstorms are expected to arrive \nTuesday morning. Elevated convection will likely be decaying as it \nmoves into western NY. Deepening wind fields and a moisture plume \nare expected across the region Tuesday. Surface based CAPE of 1000-\n2000 J/kg will provide ample instability through the afternoon and \nscattered thunderstorms are expected to form along a pre-frontal \ntrough and lake breeze boundaries late Tuesday morning through \nafternoon. The SPC has a Slight Risk for the forecast area with \ndamaging winds as the main threat. Curving hodographs in a moist \nenvironment favor some supercells and large hail and an isolated \ntornado can't be ruled out. Locally heavy rain is possible, \nwith a signal that training thunderstorms could produce \nlocalized flooding from the western Finger Lakes region to the \neastern Lake Ontario Tuesday afternoon into evening.\n\nThe warm and moist environment will persist into Tuesday evening and \nadditional showers and a few thunderstorms are possible until the \ncold front moves through overnight. \n\nKey Message 2...Cool Wednesday, with a chance of a few lingering \nshowers or thunderstorms.\n\nA closed low will set-up over southern Quebec Wednesday. A much \ncooler airmass will settle across the forecast area. High \ntemperatures will reach the upper 60s across the higher terrain to \nthe low 70s across the lake plains. Steepening low level lapse rates \nwithin a westerly flow will support scattered showers through the \nafternoon.",
